  "enhanced_information": "In a 3-week-old newborn presenting with yellowish-green vomiting and an x-ray showing no gas distal to the duodenum, there is a high suspicion of duodenal obstruction, potentially due to malrotation or atresia. The presence of bilious vomiting and the imaging findings necessitate further evaluation to confirm the diagnosis. While an emergent exploratory laparotomy is definitive for treatment, it is invasive and should be reserved for cases where non-invasive imaging fails to provide clarity. An upper gastrointestinal contrast series is the most appropriate next step as it can visualize the obstruction and is less invasive than surgery. Abdominal ultrasound may help assess for other causes but is not as specific for confirming duodenal obstruction. Flexible sigmoidoscopy is not appropriate for upper gastrointestinal issues. Therefore, the best next step in management is an upper gastrointestinal contrast series.",
